## Setup

Install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and run commands from this project folder:

```bash
uv sync
uv run pytest
uv run jupyter lab
```

This project uses Python {{ python_version }}. `uv sync` creates `.venv`, installs
the package in editable mode, and installs development tools. Select that
environment as your notebook kernel in VS Code. Imports from
`{{ project_name }}` then work without editing `sys.path`.

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` only if you need local secrets. `.env` is ignored
by Git and is **not loaded automatically**. Export variables in your shell or
explicitly use `uv run --env-file .env ...` when a command needs them.

Commit `uv.lock`, `.python-version`, and `.copier-answers.yml`. Other contributors
can use `uv sync --locked` to reproduce the recorded dependencies. Use
`uv lock --upgrade` deliberately and rerun checks after dependency updates.
Pandas is currently constrained below version 3 because UpSetPlot 0.9 plotting
fails with pandas 3's copy-on-write behavior.
PyJanitor 0.31.0 and pandas-flavor 0.7 are also constrained to keep their pandas
extension APIs compatible. Revisit these bounds together when upgrading.

## Project structure

```text
configs/                       Version-controlled experiment parameters
data/raw/                      Original, unmodified input data
data/interim/                  Intermediate transformed data
data/processed/                Model-ready datasets
data/external/                 Third-party data
models/                        Serialized models
notebooks/                     Numbered exploration notebooks
reports/figures/               Generated figures
src/{{ project_name }}/
    dataExploration/           Exploration and prediction-comparison helpers
    paths.py                   Paths independent of the working directory
    main.py                    Starter command-line entry point
tests/                         Regression tests for reusable code
```

Data, model artifacts, and generated reports are ignored by Git; `.gitkeep`
files preserve the folder structure. Track dataset sources, versions, checksums,
and acquisition steps in your project notes. Raw data should remain unchanged.
Parameters in `configs/params.yaml` are examples; the starter does not apply
them automatically.

## Explore data

Put your dataset in `data/raw/`, then use these imports in a notebook:

```python
import pandas as pd

from {{ project_name }}.dataExploration.exploration import (
    dispersion_measures_df,
    plot_central_tendency_measures,
)
from {{ project_name }}.dataExploration.missing import missing_variable_summary
from {{ project_name }}.paths import FIGURE_DIR, RAW_DATA_DIR, ensure_project_dirs

df = pd.read_csv(RAW_DATA_DIR / "dataset.csv")
summary = missing_variable_summary(df)
dispersion = dispersion_measures_df(df)
grid = plot_central_tendency_measures(df)
ensure_project_dirs()
grid.savefig(FIGURE_DIR / "distributions.png")
```

`dispersion_measures_df` summarizes numeric columns with sample variance,
quartiles, and 1.5-IQR outlier counts. The distribution plot supports numeric,
categorical, and datetime columns; it skips common identifier names and empty
columns by default. Use `exclude_columns` to omit other unwanted variables.

| Module | Useful functions |
| --- | --- |
| `dataExploration.exploration` | `dispersion_measures_df`, `encode_categories`, `decode_categories`, `plot_central_tendency_measures` |
| `dataExploration.missing` | `missing_summary`, `missing_variable_summary`, `missing_case_summary`, `missing_variable_table`, `missing_case_table`, `missing_heatmap`, `impute_knn` |
| `dataExploration.model_validation` | `models_results`, `plot_model_predictions` |

The original names `dispertion_measures_df` and `missing_heatMap` remain as
compatibility aliases. New code should use the corrected names above.

## Missing data and modeling

`impute_knn(df, exclude_columns=[...])` is an exploration helper. It fills
numeric and categorical missing values, keeps excluded columns unchanged, and
leaves datetimes and timedeltas unchanged, including `NaT`. Entirely missing
numeric or categorical columns raise an error because there are no observed
values to infer from. Column names must be unique strings. The input is not
modified; imputed numeric columns may become floats, and decoded categorical
columns do not preserve their original categorical dtype.

Its distances use unscaled values. Large-scale numeric features can dominate;
ordinal category codes impose an artificial order and are rounded after
imputation. `method="onehot"` avoids that ordinal ordering but creates dense
columns, so use care with high-cardinality data.

For model evaluation, split training and evaluation data **before fitting**
encoders, scalers, imputers, or models. Fit preprocessing on training data only
and reuse it through a scikit-learn `Pipeline`/`ColumnTransformer`. This helper
fits on each call, so it is not an inference transformer. Exclude identifiers
and targets from exploratory imputation as appropriate.

Compare predictions from already-fitted models:

```python
from {{ project_name }}.dataExploration.model_validation import (
    models_results,
    plot_model_predictions,
)

# test_df contains feature columns and a target column; model is already fitted.
results = models_results(
    test_df, [model], actual_value_col="target", model_cols=["baseline"]
)
grid = plot_model_predictions(results, actual_value_col="target")
```

The target is removed before prediction. Supply `feature_cols=[...]` when the
model expects particular features or column order. These plots compare
distributions; add task-appropriate held-out metrics and residual analysis to
assess prediction quality.

## Development

```bash
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format .
uv run pytest
uv run {{ project_name | replace('_', '-') }}
uv build
```

The command-line entry point currently creates output directories and logs
where processed data belongs. Add your pipeline as the project develops.
Keep notebooks focused on questions and findings; move reusable loading,
feature, and modeling logic into `src/{{ project_name }}/` with tests.

Paths resolve to the editable source checkout by default. For a wheel installed
elsewhere, set `DATA_PROJECT_ROOT` to an existing project directory before
importing `paths` or running the CLI; artifacts are never written into
`site-packages` by default.

To update from the template, commit your work, run `uvx copier update --trust`,
review changes and any conflicts, then rerun lint and tests. `--trust` allows
the template's `uv sync` task to execute.
